steps.body:
`while (condition) { ... }` checks the condition, runs the body if it is true, then checks again. When the condition becomes false, the loop ends and the program moves on. If it is false at the start, the body never runs.

```javascript
let count = 3;
while (count > 0) {
  console.log(count);
  count--;
}
```

Use `while` when you do not know the number of steps in advance, such as "keep growing until it passes a target". Keep a counter next to it if you need to know how many steps it took.

Gotcha: the body must change something the condition checks. Forget `count--` above and `count > 0` stays true forever, so the program never finishes. In the sandbox that shows up as a timeout.

example.code:
let population = 10;
let generations = 0;

while (population <= 1000) {
  population *= 2;
  generations++;
}

console.log(`Generations: ${generations}, population: ${population}`);

