steps.body:
A `while` loop can take a positive whole number apart one digit at a time. Two operations from Math Operators do the work:

- `n % 10` gives the last digit (4096 % 10 is 6).
- `Math.floor(n / 10)` drops the last digit (4096 becomes 409).

Repeat while `n > 0` and you visit every digit from right to left. When the last digit is dropped, `n` is 0 and the loop stops.

```javascript
let n = 4096;
while (n > 0) {
  const digit = n % 10;
  // use digit here
  n = Math.floor(n / 10);
}
```

Use `Math.floor`, not plain `/`, because `4096 / 10` is 409.6, and that leftover fraction makes every later digit wrong. The loop changes `n`, so copy the value into a `let` first if you still need the original.

To reverse a number, build it up as you go: `rev = rev * 10 + digit` shifts `rev` one place left and puts the new digit at the end. Zeros at the end of the original are lost, since a number cannot start with 0: 120 reverses to 21.

example.code:
let n = 4096;
let sum = 0;
let count = 0;
let rev = 0;

while (n > 0) {
  const digit = n % 10;
  sum += digit;
  count++;
  rev = rev * 10 + digit;
  n = Math.floor(n / 10);
}

console.log(`Digit sum: ${sum}`);
console.log(`Digits: ${count}`);
console.log(`Reversed: ${rev}`);

